5. Large Language Models — LLMs
A major class of Generative AI is the Large Language Model (LLM).

Examples include models from the GPT, Gemini, Claude, and Llama families.

An LLM is designed to work with language and generate or understand text.

Conceptually:
Text
 ↓
Tokens
 ↓
Neural Network
 ↓
Probability distribution over next token
 ↓
Next token
 ↓
Repeat
 ↓
Generated text

6. The most important idea: next-token prediction
This is an interview favorite.

Suppose the input is:
"Machine learning is"

The model estimates probabilities for possible next tokens:
a        → 0.30
important → 0.20
field      → 0.15
...

It selects a token according to the model's decoding strategy.

Then:
"Machine learning is a"

and predicts the next token again.

This happens repeatedly.

So an LLM can be described fundamentally as a system that learns to model sequences of tokens and generate them step by step.
Important correction
Don't say:
"The model thinks of the whole answer and then writes it."

A more accurate explanation is that generation proceeds autoregressively in many common LLMs, producing tokens sequentially conditioned on preceding context.
7. What is a Token?
A token is a unit of text processed by a language model.

It isn't always exactly one word.

For example:
"Hello world"

might be represented as multiple tokens depending on the tokenizer.

A long word can also be split:
unbelievable

into multiple subword pieces.

Conceptually:
Sentence
   ↓
Tokenizer
   ↓
Tokens
   ↓
Token IDs

Then the model works with those numerical IDs.
8. What is an Embedding?
An embedding is a numerical vector representation of data such as text.

For example:
"apple"
   ↓
[0.21, -0.73, 0.14, ...]

The actual vectors contain many dimensions.

The important idea is that embeddings represent semantic and contextual relationships in a numerical space.

For example, concepts with related meanings can have vectors that are closer according to a chosen similarity measure.

Embeddings are heavily used in:
- Semantic search
- Recommendation
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation
- Clustering
- Document search
9. What is a Transformer?
This is extremely important for GenAI interviews.

Modern LLMs commonly use the Transformer architecture.

The Transformer was introduced in the 2017 paper “Attention Is All You Need.”

Its central mechanism is attention.
10. What is Attention?
Attention allows the model to determine which parts of the input are important when processing a particular token.

Example:
"The animal didn't cross the road because it was tired."

What does "it" refer to?

The model needs to relate different parts of the sentence.

Attention helps model these relationships.

Conceptually:
Input tokens
     ↓
Attention
     ↓
Learn relationships between tokens
     ↓
Contextual representation

11. Self-Attention
In self-attention, tokens in a sequence attend to other tokens in that same sequence.

A simplified conceptual representation:
The     → related to → animal
it      → related to → animal
tired   → related to → was / it

The model learns these relationships through attention scores.
12. Encoder vs Decoder
Transformers can be organized differently depending on the task.
Encoder-focused models
Example:
BERT

Good for understanding/representation tasks.
Decoder-focused models
Many autoregressive LLMs use decoder-style Transformer architectures.

They are well suited for:
Text generation
Chat
Code generation

Encoder-decoder
Used for tasks such as:
Translation
Summarization
Sequence-to-sequence tasks

A good interview answer is:
Transformer architectures can be encoder-only, decoder-only, or encoder-decoder, depending on the application.

